WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.709
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.709 --> 00:00:04.500
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi,
I'm Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.660
I'm here at KubeCon EU in
Copenhagen walking the floor.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.940
And I'm here with Rani
Osnat from Aqua Security.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.710
RANI OSNAT: Hello.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.290
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: How are you?

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.050
RANI OSNAT: Great.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:12.754
Very busy.

00:00:12.754 --> 00:00:13.920
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Of course.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.700
So tell me a little
about Aqua Security.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.340
RANI OSNAT: So we provide full
lifecycle security solutions

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.470
for container and
cloud-native applications.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.470
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: What
does full lifecycle mean?

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.740
RANI OSNAT: Right, so we think
that containers change not just

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.960
how applications are
deployed, but also how

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.920
they are developed.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.200
So you need to embed security
early on in the process.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.960
So we start by integrating
through the CI/CD process

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.055
then through the registry
to deployment and runtime

00:00:38.055 --> 00:00:38.681
and production.

00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:39.721
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Got it.

00:00:39.721 --> 00:00:41.680
And this morning we had
a great announcement.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.200
We announced Cloud Security
Command Center integration

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.510
so that Aqua Security can
write findings directly to that

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.860
and customers can see that
in a single pane of glass.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.520
Tell me a little
bit more about that.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.270
RANI OSNAT: Right.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.620
So as security solutions,
we create all sorts

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.580
of events and alerts
that our customers need

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.860
to understand what's happening
in their environment,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.810
their security posture,
compliance posture,

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.130
and so forth.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:11.210
But customers seldom want yet
another dashboard to work with.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.450
So they're looking for
something more centralized.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.180
So by passing this
information, which

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.230
includes things like
vulnerability status,

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.650
runtime events in containers
that are misbehaving maybe,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.620
or images that were prevented
from running because

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.200
of security policy, we
passed all those things.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.510
In the Cloud Security Command
Center they can see all of that

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.352
in addition to other events
from GCP that they're--

00:01:37.352 --> 00:01:38.450
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: They can
look at everything happening

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.658
in their environment at once.

00:01:39.658 --> 00:01:40.630
That's great.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.380
Can we get a demo?

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.040
RANI OSNAT: Of course.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.290
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sounds good.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.515
RANI OSNAT: Right here.

00:01:44.515 --> 00:01:45.014
OK.

00:01:45.014 --> 00:01:50.020
So we're looking at the Google
Cloud Security Command Center

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.322
list of findings.

00:01:51.322 --> 00:01:52.780
And here under Aqua
Security we can

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.210
see that there are runtime
violations and vulnerability

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.122
violations, as well as image
assurance violations which

00:01:58.122 --> 00:01:59.830
means that these are
images that were not

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.550
approved by the system.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:05.110
Let's take a look at
a runtime violation.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:09.479
And there are details
here that are fed by Aqua.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.002
We can see that the action
here was detect, meaning

00:02:12.002 --> 00:02:13.210
this wasn't actually blocked.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.080
That depends on policy.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.500
And if we look at more details
on how it looks on the Aqua

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.470
console, we can see information
about the exact nature

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.760
and paths of this violation.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.940
Some environment variables.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.070
Most importantly, we can
see in the audit screen

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.870
that this was actually
an action triggered

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:39.040
by a vulnerability
known as ShellShock

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.860
in which a user tried to
do a privilege escalation

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.160
and Aqua detected
that in this case.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.910
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Thanks so much for that.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.160
That was great to see how
we detected runtime findings

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.810
and then saw them
directly in CSCC.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.450
RANI OSNAT: Yes, it's
pretty cool, isn't it?

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.500
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Thank
you so much for your time.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.360
Are you having a great
time here at KubeCon?

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.610
RANI OSNAT: Yes, it's amazing.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.890
A really amazing conference.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.500
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hopefully
we'll see you again in Seattle.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.466
RANI OSNAT: You will.

00:03:00.466 --> 00:03:03.090
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And thanks to
all those at home for watching.

